  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A cold front has just cleared the Crystal Coast\nearly this morning and will continue to push south of ENC \nthrough the day. In the wake of this front, a cooler northeast \nflow will support noticeably lower temperatures compared to what\nwe saw yesterday. Over the weekend, highs will be in the upper \n60s to near 70 degrees. Along the Outer Banks, highs may \nstruggle to reach the mid-60s. Away from bodies of water, lows \nshould fall into the 40s tonight thanks to a drier airmass and \nlighter winds. High pressure won't be directly overhead, though,\nand there will be a lot of high clouds streaming through, which\nshould prevent lows from getting as low as they otherwise \ncould. The same probably holds true for Sunday night as well.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...An amplified upper level trough is forecast to \nmove into the Central U.S. early next week, but that is where \nconfidence begins to drop. By the middle to latter half of next \nweek, medium range guidance varies drastically with the \nevolution of the upper trough as it shifts towards the Eastern \nU.S. Most guidance point towards at least a piece of the upper \ntrough getting cutoff from the flow, but where that cutoff \noccurs, and if it occurs at all, is a big question mark. \nMeanwhile, for comparison, AI guidance suggests a more \nprogressive and less amplified evolution of the trough as it \nreaches the East Coast, with less cutoff potential. \n\nWhile the upper level differences equate to much different \nsensible weather outcomes, the general theme appears to a \npattern that favors a period of southerly flow with milder \ntemperatures, increasing moisture, and the potential for showers\nand thunderstorms. The forecast is very much subject to change \nduring this timeframe, though, so stay tuned.",
